A1: TEXT PRE-PROCESSING (stop word removal + stemming)

AIM: Clean raw text so it is ready for indexing: tokenize, remove stop words, stem.

CODE EXPLAINED
1. import re / PorterStemmer
   re = regular expressions for tokenizing. PorterStemmer = NLTK's rule-based stemmer (needs no data download).

2. try: ... stopwords.words('english')
   Loads NLTK's English stop word list into a set. If NLTK data is missing it downloads it. If everything fails (offline), the except block uses a small built-in list. A set is used because lookup is O(1).

3. text = """..."""
   The input document (a paragraph).

4. tokens = re.findall(r'[a-z]+', text.lower())
   text.lower() makes everything lowercase. The regex [a-z]+ finds every run of letters, so punctuation and digits are dropped. Result is a list of words (tokenization).

5. filtered = [w for w in tokens if w not in stop_words]
   List comprehension: keep a word only if it is not a stop word (the, is, of...).

6. ps = PorterStemmer(); stemmed = [ps.stem(w) for w in filtered]
   Applies Porter suffix-stripping to each word: retrieval -> retriev, documents -> document, engines -> engin.

7. Final loop prints word -> stem side by side with zip().

PIPELINE: raw text -> lowercase -> tokens -> remove stop words -> stems.

OUTPUT TO EXPECT: tokens list, filtered list (shorter), stemmed list, and a two-column table.

VIVA Q&A
Q: Why pre-process text?
A: Raw text has noise. Cleaning shrinks the vocabulary and makes query words match document words.

Q: What are stop words? Why remove them?
A: Very common words (the, is, of) with little meaning. Removing them saves index space and improves ranking.

Q: What is stemming?
A: Reducing a word to its root (retrieval, retrieve -> retriev). The stem need not be a real word.

Q: Stemming vs lemmatization?
A: Stemming chops suffixes by rules (fast, crude). Lemmatization uses a dictionary and returns a real word (better -> good), slower.

Q: Which stemmer did you use?
A: Porter stemmer. Others: Lancaster (more aggressive), Snowball.

Q: What does re.findall(r'[a-z]+') do?
A: Extracts runs of letters only. This is tokenization.

Q: Why lowercase first?
A: So "Search" and "search" are the same term.

Q: Why a set for stop words?
A: Membership check is O(1) instead of scanning a list.

Q: Drawback of stemming?
A: Over-stemming (different words, same stem) and under-stemming. Example: "quickly" -> "quickli".

Q: What other pre-processing steps exist?
A: Removing punctuation/numbers, handling case, lemmatization, n-grams, spelling correction.

